## Reference rig: GL.iNet GL-E750 "Mudi" — SSH venue with CAPTURE-ONLY serial.
##
## Local labgrid target: NO coordinator/exporter needed (labgrid can build a
## Target in-process from YAML). Copy to a local (gitignored) file, fill in the
## placeholders for your own bench, and run with the labgrid pytest plugin or a
## small driver script.
##
## Model: models/glinet-gl-e750.json (ath79/nand, mips_24kc). Species notes:
## recipes/glinet/e750/notes.md.
##
## Only REAL labgrid classes are used here. Note that `ManualPowerPort` /
## `ManualSwitchPort` are NOT labgrid classes (checked against labgrid 26.0.1:
## resource classes are LAAPowerPort, NetworkPowerPort, NetworkYKUSHPowerPort,
## SiSPMPowerPort, TasmotaPowerPort, USBPowerPort, YKUSHPowerPort). conwrt's
## desk-rig equivalent of "a human is the power/button resource" is
## `scripts/flash/context.py::manual_step()` (docs/DEVICE-TRANSITIONS.md P1),
## which is what to call for the power step below.
##
## WHY THE POWER STEP IS MANUAL
## The GL-E750 is battery powered with no switched power and no controllable
## PoE port. Nothing in labgrid can power-cycle it, so a test that needs a cold
## boot must ask the operator (manual_step()) and then poll for the box coming
## back (SSH port / link) rather than assume it.
##
## WHY THERE IS NO SerialDriver / ShellDriver BELOW — measured, not assumed
## OUTPUT WORKS, INPUT DOES NOT. The kernel console is ttyS0 @115200 and
## /proc/consoles reports it write-only ('ttyS0 -W-'). Two closed-loop tests
## against the unit's own ttyS0 settle the direction:
##   board -> host: a marker written to /dev/kmsg over SSH arrived BYTE-CLEAN
##                  on the adapter (38 bytes). Output and host RX are fine.
##   host -> board: the unit's own UART counters DO count the bytes
##                  (/proc/tty/driver/serial: "tx:22461 rx:1785 fe:5" — 5 framing
##                  errors), but nothing is ever echoed back. CR, LF, CRLF and
##                  24+ bare CRs across two sessions produced zero returned bytes,
##                  and /sbin/askfirst never printed a prompt.
## So a ShellDriver here would block until timeout and look like a dead board.
## RawSerialPort is declared for CAPTURE ONLY — boot logs, panic output, and
## recovery evidence when SSH is down — and carries no driver. Everything that
## mutates goes over SSH.
## (An earlier revision of this file said the UART was outright 'TX-only'; that
## was wrong in the details — bytes do reach the board, framing errors are
## counted. The statement above is the operationally correct one.)
##
## If YOUR unit's wiring does accept input (verify with a loopback test first,
## per AGENTS.md "Serial Wiring: Verify Before Connecting"), then add:
##     SerialDriver: {}
##     ShellDriver:
##       prompt: "# "
##       login_prompt: "Please press Enter to activate this console"
##       username: root
## The console is askfirst — it prints nothing until it receives a bare CR
## (conwrt crates/conwrt-serial/src/watch.rs nudges it every ~10s).
##
## EXPECT ONE RADIO
## If the 5GHz QCA9887 fails to probe ("could not probe fw (-22)") there is no
## phy0 and no 5GHz interface at all — only the 2.4GHz ath9k. Do not write tests
## that assume two radios, and check `iw dev` for type: the 2.4GHz interface is
## commonly a CLIENT (STA) uplink, not an AP.
##
## Placeholders to replace for your own bench:
##   <dut-ip>        management/LAN address of the unit
##   <dut-console>   /dev/serial/by-id/... of your UART adapter — pin the FTDI
##                   SERIAL, never the ttyUSB index (the index drifts on replug)
targets:
  main:
    resources:
      NetworkService:
        address: <dut-ip>
        username: root
      RawSerialPort:
        port: <dut-console>
        speed: 115200          ## verified: ttyS0 115200 8N1
    drivers:
      SSHDriver:
        keyfile: "~/.ssh/id_ed25519"
        connection_timeout: 120.0
        explicit_scp_mode: True   ## OpenWrt dropbear has no sftp-server -> scp -O
images:
  root: null                       ## filled per-run: path to the sysupgrade image
